-- JSONDECODE of a DATETIME written as the text jl4-service answers a DATETIME
-- with, and `l4 batch` writes one as: the instant in UTC, with a space and not
-- a T before the time and the zone after it (smucclaw/l4-ide#1085). It was
-- refused ("Could not parse datetime string"), so a result of either could not
-- be fed back in as a fact.
IMPORT prelude

TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

GIVEN s IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING DATETIME
`decode` s MEANS JSONDECODE s

GIVEN s IS A STRING
GIVETH AN EITHER STRING NUMBER
`hour of` s MEANS
  CONSIDER `decode` s
    WHEN RIGHT w THEN RIGHT (TIME_HOUR (DATETIME_TIME w))
    WHEN LEFT e THEN LEFT e

-- The same instant in the three forms, read in the document's timezone
-- (Singapore is eight hours ahead of UTC): 10:30 UTC is 18:30 there.
#EVAL `hour of` "\"2026-01-31 10:30:00 UTC\""      -- RIGHT 18
#EVAL `hour of` "\"2026-01-31 10:30:00.5 UTC\""    -- RIGHT 18
#EVAL `hour of` "\"2026-01-31T10:30:00Z\""         -- RIGHT 18
#EVAL `hour of` "\"2026-01-31T18:30:00+0800\""     -- RIGHT 18

#ASSERT `decode` "\"2026-01-31 10:30:00 UTC\"" EQUALS `decode` "\"2026-01-31T10:30:00Z\""
#ASSERT `decode` "\"2026-01-31 10:30:00 UTC\"" EQUALS `decode` "\"2026-01-31T18:30:00+0800\""
